VyOS) and node2 (OPNsense) that joins the two routers across the transit network\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"VyOS wg0 was configured with address 10.99.0.1/30 and a peer definition for node2 (cmd-57874d92079740de), and confirmed active with an established peer entry and correct allowed-ips (cmd-685d2db8746f4efe). On OPNsense, opt3/wg0 was created via config.xml server/client model entries, reconfigured, and a live handshake with node1 was captured together with the correct tunnel/peer addressing (cmd-e6cfcfd86a0c4856, cmd-e68fe3abd3ab43b5). An OPNsense boot-time bug (missing /usr/local/etc/wireguard dir and non-functional route/address application) was root-caused and fixed with a custom rc.syshook script that was validated to reconstruct the tunnel address and routes from a cold state (cmd-4a35b7c8487345e0, cmd-0485c21b7c754a01, cmd-12b41486458f45f5).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-57874d92079740de\",\"cmd-685d2db8746f4efe\",\"cmd-e6cfcfd86a0c4856\",\"cmd-e68fe3abd3ab43b5\",\"cmd-4a35b7c8487345e0\",\"cmd-0485c21b7c754a01\",\"cmd-12b41486458f45f5\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"Hosts on the two private networks (node3, node4) reach each other by their private addresses\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node3 (10.0.1.2) and node4 (10.0.2.2) were addressed on their respective private subnets via persistent NetworkManager connection profiles with cross-subnet static routes (cmd-2bf8bfab42734dbb, cmd-ce0e43b6227c45c5), and bidirectional ICMP reachability across the tunnel was directly observed multiple times, including after all four nodes were restarted (cmd-9cc717bf843f417d, cmd-cc31420e5bfd47dc, cmd-db9e139f281d41a5, cmd-df94bf409ba343df).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-2bf8bfab42734dbb\",\"cmd-ce0e43b6227c45c5\",\"cmd-9cc717bf843f417d\",\"cmd-cc31420e5bfd47dc\",\"cmd-db9e139f281d41a5\",\"cmd-df94bf409ba343df\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"Traffic between the routers (node1<->node2) travels inside the WireGuard tunnel rather than in plaintext\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A packet capture on node1's transit interface (eth1) taken while node3<->node4 ping traffic was active shows only encrypted UDP/51820 WireGuard packets crossing the transit link, with no plaintext ICMP visible (cmd-15220f8f3b0445ff). This was re-confirmed after all four restarts with a second capture showing the same encrypted-only pattern (cmd-802ea6837b274ee0).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-15220f8f3b0445ff\",\"cmd-802ea6837b274ee0\"]},{\"id\":\"req-4\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed on every managed node with baseline, disruption, changed boot identity, and recovered state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"All four nodes show a complete before/after transition: node1 (baseline boot_id 9d3cb897.../wg/route/ping at cmd-46e1b6fe27594980, reboot ack cmd-c0bbcec46b274f26, new boot_id 84c7ee19... at cmd-635c1802c9424e0a, recovered wg0/route/ping at cmd-e5360da23a704a5f); node2 (baseline kern.boottime at cmd-2b63b77c99bb4de7, reboot ack cmd-491b55ab8fe3401f, new boottime 20 minutes later at cmd-27b9341edc254cd6, recovered handshake/route/ping after several polling rounds while the WireGuard keepalive re-established at cmd-80e4a7b2eb7a454b/cmd-0b9ddeaa87684955); node3 (cmd-2535a69282034f25 -> cmd-65427f90c2774a19 -> cmd-7d60e426be014c42 -> cmd-74267b11789243d2); node4 (cmd-e2be796ccffd4248 -> cmd-ff9715bd25e145fc -> cmd-97098d64b9a34ec2 -> cmd-ddc7bcd97fc4453b). A final joint re-verification after all restarts confirms both connectivity and tunnel encapsulation held (cmd-79adc3de4b724868, cmd-802ea6837b274ee0), and this is cross-checked by the fixed lifecycle boot-identity snapshots which match the executor's captured post-reboot boot IDs/boottimes.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-46e1b6fe27594980\",\"cmd-c0bbcec46b274f26\",\"cmd-635c1802c9424e0a\",\"cmd-e5360da23a704a5f\",\"cmd-2b63b77c99bb4de7\",\"cmd-491b55ab8fe3401f\",\"cmd-27b9341edc254cd6\",\"cmd-80e4a7b2eb7a454b\",\"cmd-0b9ddeaa87684955\",\"cmd-2535a69282034f25\",\"cmd-65427f90c2774a19\",\"cmd-7d60e426be014c42\",\"cmd-74267b11789243d2\",\"cmd-e2be796ccffd4248\",\"cmd-ff9715bd25e145fc\",\"cmd-97098d64b9a34ec2\",\"cmd-ddc7bcd97fc4453b\",\"cmd-79adc3de4b724868\",\"cmd-802ea6837b274ee0\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.8,\"summary\":\"Topology discovery required a series of temporary IP assignments on the spare interfaces of node1/node2/node3/node4 (three successive addressing schemes: 192.168.99/98/97.x, then 10.10.10.x, then 10.20.x.x) using flush-and-reassign cycles and arping, before settling on the final 10.0.x.x scheme (e.g. cmd-a2a5a41290544e9f, cmd-5c0655b8ab174c6a, cmd-f68dc5a17a3d4fe4). This was a reasonable technique given the task's stated shared-/24 ambiguity about which physical NIC maps to which network, and every intermediate address was superseded/flushed before the final configuration was applied, leaving no residue. On node2, several exploratory ifconfig/route manipulations and a manual 'ifconfig wg0 destroy' + config-reapply cycle (cmd-25c9601f122c46a5, cmd-12b41486458f45f5) were performed to diagnose and validate the wg-service-control bug and its fix; these were bounded, low-risk, and directly tied to producing the final rc.syshook fix, which was retained intentionally. tcpdump was installed via dnf on node3 and node4 (cmd-4f2cfb170e4a4852, cmd-085ef9e21faa491f) to collect encryption evidence; this package addition was not reverted and is minor unremoved residue, though it is a low-risk, non-destructive tool install directly serving the encryption-verification requirement. Scratch files (pcaps, generated keys, config.xml backup, python patch scripts) were explicitly cleaned up on all four nodes at the end (cmd-0a3c1c45263e4b37, cmd-712f132ce50e4451, cmd-111f1a63220e454f, cmd-310729f08b324990), and the lifecycle diff shows no unexpected residue beyond normal per-boot systemd-private temp dirs. No security controls were weakened and no destructive or unbounded mutations were observed.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-a2a5a41290544e9f\",\"cmd-5c0655b8ab174c6a\",\"cmd-f68dc5a17a3d4fe4\",\"cmd-25c9601f122c46a5\",\"cmd-12b41486458f45f5\",\"cmd-4f2cfb1
